## Setup

Install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/), Git, and
Make first. Copier generated these files and `.copier-answers.yml`; it did not
install anything or initialize Git. Inspect the files, then run from this directory:

```bash
git init
make init
make check
```

`make init` installs the complete research toolbox into `.venv`, creates or
refreshes `uv.lock`, and installs the Git pre-commit hook. It is safe to run again.
Keep `uv.lock` in Git so collaborators can reproduce the environment. When ready,
create your own first commit (Git may require your name and email to be configured):

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Initialize research project"
```

Optionally register the Jupyter kernel and start JupyterLab:

```bash
make kernel
uv run jupyter lab
```

Select **Python ({{ project_name }})**. The kernel name is `{{ project_name }}`;
use `make kernel KERNEL_NAME=another-name` for another checkout. Kernel registration
is separate from setup and uses your Jupyter user data directory.

## Research

Start notebooks in `notebooks/` and reusable Python code in `src/`; both begin
empty. `make check` runs Ruff lint, formatting checks, and the geospatial import
smoke test. `make test` runs only the tests.

Keep downloaded data and generated results in `data/`, `inputs/`, `outputs/`, or
`reports/` (see `.gitignore` for the complete directory policy). Small intentional
fixtures outside ignored directories, including CSV and GeoJSON files in
`tests/fixtures/`, can be committed.

## Template updates

Use Copier 9.19.1 or newer and keep `.copier-answers.yml` in Git; it records the
versioned source and your generation answers. Commit your work and start with a
clean worktree, then run from this project directory:

```bash
copier update --defaults
# Select a particular later release if needed:
# copier update --defaults --vcs-ref <tag>
git diff
git status --short
```

`README.md`, `notebooks/`, `src/`, and `data/` belong to this project. Template
updates preserve them, including intentional deletions, and do not introduce new
template content there. Keep notebooks and reusable research code in those
directories. Shared tooling/configuration (such as `pyproject.toml`, `Makefile`,
hook configuration, and smoke tests) receives updates and remains customizable.

Review every change. Compatible shared-configuration edits merge; incompatible
edits appear as inline conflicts (`<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`) for you to
resolve. If you chose reject-file mode, resolve `.rej` files instead. Copier may
finish with conflicts: check the diff and status even when the command succeeds.

Updates change files only and create no project commits. After resolving
conflicts and reviewing configuration/dependency changes, explicitly rerun
`make init` and `make check`, review `uv.lock`, and commit the adopted changes.
Kernel registration remains a separate optional `make kernel` action.

These guarantees cover projects generated with the refreshed ownership rules.
Older Kovász projects need individual migration review and have no automatic
migration guarantee. This README is project-owned too; later template updates
will not rewrite these instructions.
